WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.030 align:start position:0%
 
the climate activist group extinction  rebellion has kicked off worldwide  protests warning of an environmental  apocalypse here in Berlin protesters  gathered at dawn to block streets in the  city's center over the next two weeks  civil disobedience actions where  protesters attempt to peacefully  paralyze cities are planned in 60 cities  around the world protesters want  greenhouse gas emissions to be cut to  zero by 2025 we now go to our  correspondence in London and here in  Berlin let's start with Beckett Moss who  is joining us there on the streets of  London I understand that protests are  just getting underway there what's the  situation on the streets well I'm here  in the government district and London  and protesters are blocking several  sites in and around the government  district they are trying to block it  what you can see going on behind me is  that protesters have been trying to put  up tents and other structures and police  have then calmly taken the same  structures down so it seems a little bit  like a back end force  so what protesters try to put things up  and then again police put things down  I've also seen one young gentleman  arrested and there's been media reports  that these there have been so far 21  arrests all over London now the last  time there was a big protest in London  was at the beginning of the summer  before the summer and police have not  been very heavy-handed however today  I've seen here in London a lot of police  on the streets and they have also  announced that they will arrest people  so the question is how many protesters  will want to be arrested or will will be  willing to be arrested but it seems that  it's just going underway and it's a bit  lava you know a bit of a battle but a  very peaceful battle at the moment  between what the protesters won and what  the police want okay a number of arrests  already in Berlin or rather in London  and a massive police presence so Simon  young is our correspondents on the  street here in Berlin Simon what are you  seeing there  Terry I'm standing here at the victory  monument that's not far from the  Brandenburg Gate in the center of Berlin  it's a key traffic roundabout and the  protest is several hundred of them  who've been here since the early morning  have managed to block traffic from  passing through here I can show you the  scene it's an empty street normally this  would be full of heavy traffic buses  lorries and cars at this time of day  today there's nothing and also the  atmosphere here rather different to what  bigots describing in London there have  been no arrests the police are holding  off they're taking a watch and watch and  wait approach and the atmosphere among  protesters is peaceful nonviolent that  people are singing songs and chanting  slogans and you probably see behind me  there they've been building this sort of  Noah's Ark that they've been painting  pink that's going to be the central  focus of the demonstrations here today  now these protests have been anticipated  of course we caught up with some of the  activists in the German capital here as  they made their preparations for this  week of protests let's take a look this  is how environmental activists from  extinction rebellion are preparing for  the protests one of them is 28 year old  Luca Alice the activists undergo  training and how to behave when blocking  a road and when being removed by the  police  our Liz and the other activists are  demanding more climate protection action  from governments that includes a call  for a climate emergency to be declared  to make their point activists want a  paralyzed transport in Berlin Luka Alice  says she's willing to be arrested if  necessary this is the only way to show  how serious we are it's not sufficient  anymore to simply wave flags and  demonstrate we need to make clear how  bad the situation is  and I think it will be highly symbolic  if we are willing to be arrested and  have our freedom curtailed extinction  rebellion was founded in 2018 and claims  to have activists in around 50 countries  their protest method is civil  disobedience peaceful but sometimes  illegal and provocative to illustrate  the effects of climate change the  activists even died River in Switzerland  Green  the dye was harmless but the campaign  got a lot of attention and polarized  opinion the protesters have pitched  their tents in Berlin not just anywhere  but right in the heart of the government  district their expected roadblocks have  provoked mixed reactions from Berliners  I think it's bad we need to find a  sensible solution a broad consensus in  society is what we're after and  extremism doesn't help I don't know what  they're going to achieve apart from  creating more traffic which isn't very  climate friendly  they should do it it's not a problem we  need to do something about it don't we  extinction rebellion only released a  partial list of which parts of Berlin it  intends to shut down the protest will  last a week but for many the climate  fighters a long-term commitment Alice  who previously worked as a psychologist  has decided to live off her savings and  dedicate yourself full-time to the  campaign pursuing a career is becoming  more and more irrelevant if you look at  the broad context our work here is very  important so I can't imagine doing  anything else  at this moment [ __ ] America Lucca alles  dream is that soon governments around  the world will listen to her and her  peers and it's that goal that extinction  rebellion want to raise awareness for  over the coming days  Birgit this isn't the first time that  extinction rebellion has staged protests  in London  what do Londoners make of it well I've  been speaking to people that are passing  by this particular protest and  admittedly very randomly I have stopped  passers-by have stopped cyclists and my  opinion is my impression is that most  are broadly supportive particularly the  people who are cycling past this is a  very very it's a route that's used often  by cyclists in the morning in and out of  work one of the big sort of routes into  the city and the cyclists are very  supportive of the process however I've  also speaking to some others and one  lady said she she does understand where  they're coming from but on the other  hand they are blocking the routes and of  course London is a very busy city and  they are blocking major traffic arteries  and and they would also that would  hinder people also who are already  traveling in a greener way for example  by buses so some people do do object to  the mess that methods that's for sure  bigger thank you very much for now  that's big at mass force in London and  here in Berlin we had Simon Young our  political correspondent out on the  streets thank you to you thanks to you  both very much  and for more on the protest we're joined  by dr. claire wordly she says wall  artist and research associate at the  University of Cambridge and she's also  an active supporter of extinction  rebellion good to have you with us first  of all dr. wordly tell us in a nutshell  what extinction rebellion is and what  makes it different from other movements  like grete tune berg's Friday for future  thank you well to begin with of course  we share a lot of the same aims as  Fridays for future which is to get the  world to take serious action to get  governments to take action on climate  change and on other ecological crises  but with its Friday's for future this is  initially this is just schoolchildren  and this is there's not much role for  adults there other than coordinating so  with extinction rebellion this is  adults saying we don't just leave this  to the to the children we are also going  to protest and it's a grassroots  movement it's decentralized meaning that  anyone can if they abide by the  principles they can call themselves  extinction rebellion they can go  forwards we have a lot of connections  across all the different countries we  speak with each other but we're not for  example told what to do by the London  extinction rebellion or so on Berlin  takes its own actions and we make our  points by using civil disobedience okay  you this week your group of staging a  series of demonstrations all around the  world 60 cities I understand including  Berlin what are you hoping to achieve  with these actions so yeah so we've  already kicked off in Berlin with a  protest which started at 4:00 a.m. this  morning and we're hoping to do a variety  of things firstly to show the public how  absolutely serious these crises are I  think it's very easy to forget about  climate change and ecological collapse  because they're not very nice things to  think about we don't want to read about  them all the time and by disrupting we  bring it to the forefront of people's  attention we also bring it to the  forefront of governments attention and  show them that the people that the  citizens are not going to take this  lying down that we we want stronger  action that we have been trying for many  years to get this through different  means and the now the time has come  where we actually go out and we stand in  the streets and stay there until action  is taken so you're trying to draw  attention to the urgency of this the  language that you use it goes in the  direction of apocalypse we need to be  and something needs to be done now how  far given the urgency of it do you think  it's reasonable to push your agenda what  are the limits of civil disobedience for  you so for extinction rebellion as a  whole we have a set of guiding  principles one of those very important  principles is non-violence so we would  never say that violence in any form is  ever justified so we we remain within  the scope of non-violent actions so for  example this includes things like  roadblocks doing ourselves to particular  buildings or particular companies or  particular ministries or departments  and causing disruption but in a in a  quite usually quite a playful manner I'm  not causing any harm to any living thing  do you have faith in liberal democracy  in like we have here in Europe for the  most part to achieve the what you  believe is necessary in order to avoid  the collapse of our society in our  ecosystem it's a very interesting  question so I think one thing is that I  do have faith in democracy and I do  believe in democracy but I think that in  many cases our democracy has subverted  by special interest so if you look at  for example the influence of lobbyists  in the US oil and gas industries spent  over 120 million in 2018 alone lobbying  the government here in Europe we have  lobbyists from the oil and gas  industries from the coal industry from  the automobile industry and these  industries actually take rights away  from citizens so we see across Europe  most people want environmental  protections in the polls that have been  conducted and but I think that democracy  needs to be a true democracy and go back  more to the people and that's why  extinction rebellion calls for citizens  assemblies with randomly chosen citizens  informed by science to take decisions  thank you so much dr. claire wordly  she's with extinction rebellion she's a  zoologist a researcher at the University  of Cambridge thanks for being with us  this morning thank you very much

